<h2 id="faq-heading">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
<div class="usa-accordion usa-accordion--bordered usa-accordion--multiselectable" data-allow-multiple="">
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="true" aria-controls="faq-1">What is slug loading?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-1" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Slug loading groups a counted number of products into a tight linear batch (slug) and feeds it into a case packer, tray loader, or shrink wrapper. The slug conveyor counts products, accumulates them against a stop gate, and releases the group synchronized to the machine's cycle.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-2">How does a slug conveyor form slugs?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-2" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Three steps: (1) Count products via photoeye or encoder. (2) Accumulate against a stop gate or slow-speed zone until count is reached. (3) Release by retracting the gate and accelerating the slug zone. A speed differential creates a gap between the departing slug and the next forming slug.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-3">What is the difference between slug loading and collating?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-3" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Slug loading groups products in a single row. <a href="/conveyor-functions/collating-laning/">Collating</a> arranges products into multi-row patterns (e.g., 3x4 grids). Many case packing operations combine both, slugs form individual rows, then collating arranges multiple slugs side-by-side into the final case pattern.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-4">What conveyor types are used for slug loading?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-4" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Belt conveyors with pneumatic or servo stop gates (most common), <a href="/conveyor-types/mdr-conveyors/">MDR conveyors</a> with zone speed control (gateless), and timing screws for round products (bottles, cans). Belt-with-gate is the most versatile. MDR is cleanest for cases and cartons. Timing screws are best for high-speed round container lines.</p>

</div>
